narrative:
## Building a Mini Order Tracker

An order needs two things: a struct to store the data (ID, amount) and an enum to represent its status (Pending, Shipped, Delivered, Cancelled).

### The Status Enum

```xiom
enum OrderStatus {
  Pending,
  Shipped,
  Delivered,
  Cancelled,
}
```

### The Order Struct

```xiom
type Order = {
  id: Int;
  amount: Float64;
  status: OrderStatus;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let order1 = Order{ id: 1001, amount: 29.99, status: OrderStatus.Pending };
  io.println(order1.id);
  return 0;
}
```

Notice: a struct field can be an enum type! `status: OrderStatus` means the status field must be one of the four OrderStatus variants.

### Methods to Update Status

```xiom
fn Order.ship(self) -> Bool {
  if status == OrderStatus.Pending {
    status = OrderStatus.Shipped;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

fn Order.deliver(self) -> Bool {
  if status == OrderStatus.Shipped {
    status = OrderStatus.Delivered;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

fn Order.cancel(self) -> Bool {
  if status == OrderStatus.Pending {
    status = OrderStatus.Cancelled;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let order = Order{ id: 1001, amount: 29.99, status: OrderStatus.Pending };

  order.ship();
  io.println("Status after ship: Shipping");

  order.deliver();
  io.println("Status after deliver: Delivered");
  return 0;
}
```

### Displaying the Order

```xiom
fn Order.describe(self) -> Str {
  let status_text = match status {
    OrderStatus.Pending => "Pending",
    OrderStatus.Shipped => "Shipped",
    OrderStatus.Delivered => "Delivered",
    OrderStatus.Cancelled => "Cancelled",
  };
  return "Order #" + " [" + status_text + "] $";
}
```

### Processing Multiple Orders

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let o1 = Order{ id: 1001, amount: 29.99, status: OrderStatus.Pending };
  let o2 = Order{ id: 1002, amount: 45.00, status: OrderStatus.Pending };

  o1.ship();
  o2.cancel();

  o1.describe();
  o2.describe();
  return 0;
}
```

### Why This Combination Is Powerful

The struct holds what doesn't change (ID, amount). The enum tracks what does change (status). Methods enforce the rules (no shipping a Cancelled order). Together, they create a robust system.

